{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba collab/1789222642509-architect-macro-system-v2-design.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-12 14:18:45 GMT\n     2\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-12 21:18:45 +07\n     3\tCoding-Agent: claude\n     4\tSession-ID: fc5dc6a9-b701-4ba8-b862-8dad76ada2e3\n     5\t\n     6\t# Unified compile-time and runtime macros on yin.vm — architectural specification\n     7\t\n     8\tRole: Lead System Architect. Scope: design only; no repository file other\n     9\tthan this one was changed.\n    10\t\n    11\tCompanion to `collab/1789221648668-architect-semantic-vm-v2-design.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`\n    12\t(the semantic VM), which this document cites as \"the semantic spec\". Where\n    13\tthe two disagree, the later document (this one) states the reconciliation\n    14\texplicitly in Appendix A.\n    15\t\n    16\t## 0. Summary of decisions\n    17\t\n    18\t1. **One expander, three phases of use.** `yin.vm.macro` is a single\n    19\t   pure engine over canonical `:yin/*` datoms. It runs ahead of time as a\n    20\t   batch-to-batch pass (compile time), and it is called at a boundary by each\n    21\t   evaluator (runtime). There is no second implementation of expansion\n    22\t   anywhere; evaluators own only the *splice* of an expansion back into their\n    23\t   own control representation.\n    24\t2. **A macro body is executed by the reference evaluator on AST, always.** A\n    25\t   macro lambda is a Universal AST lambda. Its body runs on a throwaway\n    26\t   `yin.vm.ast-walker` instance, whether the surrounding program is being\n    27\t   expanded ahead of time, or is running on the walker, the semantic VM, or a\n    28\t   future stack VM. Macro bodies are small, rare, and their semantics *are*\n    29\t   AST semantics; lowering them buys nothing and would put a second evaluator\n    30\t   on the path of every expansion. The evaluator used for macro bodies is\n    31\t   supplied by the composition (`:macro-eval`), never resolved from a global.\n    32\t3. **Macro arguments are AST values, not entity ids.** A macro receives its\n    33\t   operands as Universal AST maps bound to its parameters (variadic `&`\n    34\t   supported), and returns a Universal AST map. v1 passed entity ids and a\n    35\t   `get-attr` closure; that coupled macro bodies to a datom index and made\n    36\t   them unwritable from a non-Clojure frontend. Maps are what every frontend\n    37\t   already produces and what every evaluator already consumes. Unchanged\n    38\t   operand subtrees are still *shared*, not copied, because reconstructed\n    39\t   operand maps carry their `:eid` and the emitter references an existing\n    40\t   entity instead of re-emitting it (§3.5).\n    41\t4. **Expansion is outermost-first and re-expands its own output to a\n    42\t   fixpoint.** A macro sees its operands unexpanded (so a macro can rewrite\n    43\t   forms that themselves contain macro calls), and the expander then walks the\n    44\t   returned AST and expands whatever it contains. Fixpoint is reached when the\n    45\t   output tree contains no `:yin/macro-expand` node whose macro is enabled for\n    46\t   the current phase. Depth (nesting of expansion inside expansion output) is\n    47\t   guarded at 100 and emitted datoms per expansion transaction at 10,000.\n    48\t5. **Compile-time output is 100% macro-free for the current phase, and the\n    49\t   root is an explicit fact.** The expanded batch carries\n    50\t   `[root :yin/root true]`, and `yin.vm/index-datoms` honours it. Without\n    51\t   it the existing \"last unreferenced typed entity\" heuristic would select the\n    52\t   *unexpanded* original root, because expansion is non-destructive and the\n    53\t   original root stays in the batch. Every consumer (walker loader,\n    54\t   linearizer, future stack compiler) reads the same fact.\n    55\t6. **Runtime expansion is a transition that ends by splicing, and its record\n    56\t   is data on the VM value.** In the walker the spliced thing is an AST node\n    57\t   (`cesk-return state expanded-ast env k nil`). In the semantic VM it is an\n    58\t   ephemeral segment lowered on the spot and *called* with a return frame. In\n    59\t   both, the expansion event and its output datoms are appended to a\n    60\t   `:macro-expansions` ledger on the VM value, which the composition drains to\n    61\t   any stream it chooses, exactly as the REPL drains output. Nothing about an\n    62\t   expansion is hidden in a host object.\n    63\t7. **Authority is supplied, not assumed.** Runtime expansion consults a\n    64\t   composition-supplied `:macro-authorize` function with the phase, macro\n    65\t   entity, program root and the presented capability. Absent authorizer means\n    66\t   *deny*: a runtime macro on a VM built without one is a hard error, never a\n    67\t   silent expansion. Compile-time expansion is allowed by default because the\n    68\t   build context is trusted; a composition may still restrict it.\n    69\t8. **A macro closure applied as a function is an error.** `:lambda` nodes\n    70\t   carrying `:yin/macro?` produce closure values carrying `:macro?`, and\n    71\t   `apply-function` refuses them. A missed expansion therefore fails loudly\n    72\t   rather than evaluating operands that were meant to be syntax.\n    73\t\n    74\tThe rest of this document states the contracts these decisions imply.\n    75\t\n    76\t---\n    77\t\n    78\t## §1. Foundational axioms and invariant governance\n    79\t\n    80\t### 1.1 The three axioms the brief names, plus the fourth\n    81\t\n    82\t**Interpretation creates semantics.** A `:yin/macro-expand` datom is syntax.\n    83\t`yin.vm.macro/expand` is the interpreter that gives it the meaning \"replace\n    84\tme with what this lambda returns when handed my operands as data\". The same\n    85\tdatom read by a query means \"a call site of macro *m*\"; read by a renderer it\n    86\tmeans the original source form; read by `linearize` under a `:runtime` policy\n    87\tit means \"emit a `:macro-call` instruction\". The macro lambda itself is an\n    88\tordinary `:lambda` entity whose only difference from a function is two facts\n    89\t(`:yin/macro?`, `:yin/phase-policy`), and it is the *expander's* reading of\n    90\tthose facts — not anything in the walker's closure application — that makes\n    91\tit a macro. The expander runs the body with the walker and interprets the\n    92\treturned value as an AST; the walker does not know it is expanding anything.\n    93\t\n    94\t**Code and state are datoms.** Macro definitions are `:lambda` entities.\n    95\tCall sites are `:yin/macro-expand` entities with `:yin/operator` and\n    96\t`:yin/operands` refs. Every expansion produces a `:macro-expand-event`\n    97\tentity and output entities with `m` = the event eid, appended to the same\n    98\tbounded batch (compile time) or to the VM's expansion ledger (runtime). The\n    99\toriginal call datoms are never retracted or rewritten; the expanded program\n   100\tis a *new* root that shares every unchanged subtree by reference. A query can\n   101\ttherefore answer \"what did this call expand to\", \"which macro produced this\n   102\tnode\", and \"what did the program look like before expansion\" from the facts,\n   103\twithout the expander.\n   104\t\n   105\t**Everything is a stream.** Compile-time expansion is an interpreter in the\n   106\tsense of `dao.stream.md` §Composition: it reads one batch and produces one\n   107\tbatch, and lives entirely outside the stream contract. It is composed either\n   108\tinto a loader (`(comp vm-load-program macro/expand-batch)`) or as a forwarder\n   109\tstep between a program-in and a program-out medium (§3.7). It is not\n   110\tper-datom and not per-node: a batch is the unit, as `streams-all-the-way-down.md`\n   111\t§4 requires. Runtime expansion is a boundary: it ends the current transition\n   112\twith a record appended to the ledger and control transferred to the splice.\n   113\tThe ledger is drained by the composition as a batch; no callback, no host\n   114\tobject, no stream handle inside the transition (§4.4 says why the transition\n   115\tdoes not append to a stream itself).\n   116\t\n   117\t**Everything is a continuation.** A runtime expansion in the walker is\n   118\t`cesk-return` with the expanded AST as control and the *same* `env` and `k`;\n   119\ta parked continuation captured after an expansion names AST that is fully\n   120\tpresent in the VM value. In the semantic VM an expansion is a segment call: the\n   121\tframe pushed is `{:segment :pc :env :stack-base}` like any other, and the\n   122\tephemeral segment is stored under `:code` by id, so a shipped continuation\n   123\tresolves it the way it resolves any segment (semantic spec §1.1). Nothing\n   124\tabout a macro escapes into a host closure.\n   125\t\n   126\t### 1.2 The six invariants\n   127\t\n   128\t| Invariant | How the design honours it |\n   129\t|---|---|\n   130\t| No hidden global state | No registry, no `defonce`. The macro environment is a value threaded through `yang.*/compile-program` and `macro/expand`. The bootstrap macros (`defmacro`) are a value in `yin.vm.macro` passed in `opts`; the evaluator for macro bodies is `:macro-eval`, supplied at construction; the authorizer is `:macro-authorize`, supplied at construction. Fresh entity ids and gensym symbols come from counters threaded in the expansion context, seeded from the batch, not from a host counter. |\n   131\t| No implicit control flow | Expansion happens at exactly two named places: the `expand-batch` pass and the `:yin/macro-expand` transition (walker) or `:macro-call` instruction (semantic). Nothing expands lazily on lookup, on load, or on `apply`. A macro-flagged closure reaching `apply-function` is an error, not an implicit expansion. |\n   132\t| No callbacks | The expander returns data. Runtime expansion records data on the VM. The composition drains the ledger with an ordinary step it owns. The macro body evaluator is a function *supplied to* the VM, called synchronously as a subroutine on a throwaway VM value; it cannot call back into the executing VM because it is never given it. |\n   133\t| No shared mutable state | The expansion context `{:datoms :by-entity :next-eid :next-sym :depth :emitted}` is a persistent map threaded through `loop`/`reduce`. The one local mutable cell — the gensym counter inside a single macro-body invocation (§2.4) — is created per invocation, closed over only by that invocation's `yin/gensym-sym` primitive, and unreachable after it returns. The throwaway walker used for a macro body is a fresh value with a fresh store. |\n   134\t| No layer collapsing | Four functions with data between each: *compile* (yang: source → AST), *expand* (`macro`: datoms → datoms), *lower/load* (`linearize`, `vm-load-program`), *execute*. Runtime expansion re-enters *expand* as a subroutine from *execute* and re-enters *lower* for the semantic VM, but each remains a separate function over data; the executing VM never interprets the macro body and the expander never touches VM control state. |\n   135\t| No assumed graphs | The expander constructs its index from the batch with `index-datoms` in an explicit pass; the root is the explicit `:yin/root` fact; a `:yin/operator` that resolves to no macro is an error naming the call eid; a `:macro-call` instruction whose macro cannot be resolved at run time is an error naming the pc and the source eid. |\n   136\t\n   137\t---\n   138\t\n   139\t## §2. Macro representation and language integration\n   140\t\n   141\t### 2.1 The macro entity\n   142\t\n   143\tUnchanged from `docs/design/macro-design.md` and already round-tripped by\n   144\t`yin.vm/ast->datoms` and `datoms->ast`:\n   145\t\n   146\t| Attribute | Value | Note |\n   147\t|---|---|---|\n   148\t| `:yin/type` | `:lambda` | a macro is structurally a lambda |\n   149\t| `:yin/params` | vector of symbols, `&` permitted before the last | variadic binding is the expander's, §2.4 |\n   150\t| `:yin/body` | ref | |\n   151\t| `:yin/macro?` | `true` | required on a macro; absent on a function |\n   152\t| `:yin/phase-policy` | `:compile` \\| `:runtime` \\| `:both` | required when `:yin/macro?` is true; `ast->datoms` defaults it to `:compile` |\n   153\t\n   154\tAST map form: `{:type :lambda :params [...] :body ... :macro? true\n   155\t:phase-policy :compile}`, with an optional `:eid` when the frontend\n   156\tpre-allocates an id (yang.clojure does, so the `yin/def` operand and every\n   157\tcall-site operator that embeds the same map become one entity).\n   158\t\n   159\t### 2.2 The call node\n   160\t\n   161\t```\n   162\t{:type :yin/macro-expand\n   163\t :operator <macro-ast>        ; a :lambda map (macro? true) or a :variable map\n   164\t :operands [<ast> ...]        ; unevaluated Universal AST nodes\n   165\t :tail? true|false}           ; as yang marks it\n   166\t```\n   167\t\n   168\tDatoms: `:yin/type :yin/macro-expand`, `:yin/operator ref`, `:yin/operands\n   169\t[ref ...]`, optional `:yin/tail?`. The operator is either the macro lambda\n   170\tentity itself (the eid *is* the macro) or a `:variable` naming it; §2.7\n   171\tdefines resolution for the second form.\n   172\t\n   173\t### 2.3 Macro values at run time\n   174\t\n   175\tTwo additions so that runtime resolution of a `:variable` operator works\n   176\tthrough the ordinary store, with no macro registry:\n   177\t\n   178\t1. **The walker's `:lambda` transition copies the flags into the closure.**\n   179\t   `{:type :closure :params :body :env}` becomes\n   180\t   `{:type :closure :params :body :env :macro? true :phase-policy p :eid e}`\n   181\t   when the node is a macro (three `assoc`s taken only on macro lambdas; the\n   182\t   hot path for functions is untouched). `:eid` is present because the\n   183\t   walker's loader keeps `:eid` on macro lambda nodes only (§3.5 on\n   184\t   `datoms->ast` with `:keep-eids`), so runtime provenance can name the macro\n   185\t   entity.\n   186\t2. **`apply-function` rejects `:macro?` closures** with\n   187\t   `ex-info \"Macro applied as a function\" {:macro eid :name sym}`. This is the\n   188\t   guard behind decision 8.\n   189\t\n   190\t`(yin/def name <macro-lambda>)` therefore puts a macro closure in the store\n   191\tunder `name`, and a later `:yin/macro-expand` whose operator is\n   192\t`{:variable name}` resolves it with `engine/resolve-var` exactly like any\n   193\tvariable. The semantic VM's `:closure` instruction carries the same three\n   194\tfacts as operand attributes (`:yin.code/macro?`, `:yin.code/phase-policy`,\n   195\t`:yin.code/macro-ast`) — §4.2 explains why the last one is inlined.\n   196\t\n   197\t### 2.4 The argument protocol and the macro prelude\n   198\t\n   199\t**Binding.** `(bind-params params operand-asts)` produces the env for the\n   200\tmacro body: positional params bind one AST map each; `& rest` binds a vector\n   201\tof the remaining AST maps; arity mismatch is a hard expansion error naming\n   202\tthe call eid (`{:kind :arity :expected n :got m}`). This binding is the\n   203\texpander's, so the walker's `zipmap` closure application (no `&`) is not\n   204\tinvolved and does not change.\n   205\t\n   206\t**Return.** The body's value must be a Universal AST map. `macro/valid-ast?`\n   207\twalks it and requires: every node a map with a keyword `:type` in the\n   208\tcanonical set (`:literal :variable :lambda :application :if\n   209\t:dao.stream.apply/call :vm/* :stream/* :yin/macro-expand`); ref-valued keys\n   210\t(`:body :operator :test :consequent :alternate :target :val :source`) hold\n   211\tmaps; `:operands` a vector of maps; `:params` a vector of symbols. Any\n   212\tother value is a hard error (`{:kind :invalid-output}`) — no coercion of a\n   213\tbare number or symbol into a literal, because \"no silent fallback\" is the\n   214\trule and the prelude makes constructing the right node one call.\n   215\t\n   216\t**The prelude** is the set of primitives merged into the throwaway VM's\n   217\t`:primitives` for the duration of one macro-body invocation. They are pure\n   218\tconstructors and accessors over AST maps:\n   219\t\n   220\t| Primitive | Meaning |\n   221\t|---|---|\n   222\t| `yin/node-type ast` | `(:type ast)` |\n   223\t| `yin/node-get ast k` | `(get ast k)`; the only accessor a macro needs |\n   224\t| `yin/literal v`, `yin/variable sym` | leaf constructors |\n   225\t| `yin/lambda params body`, `yin/application op operands`, `yin/if t c a` | node constructors; `params` is a vector of symbols |\n   226\t| `yin/sequence-body [ast ...]` | the `do` desugaring yang uses (nested `(fn [_] ...)` applications); empty → `nil` literal |\n   227\t| `yin/make-lambda params-ast body-ast` | compatibility with the v1 stdlib: `params-ast` is a `:literal` whose value is the param vector; marks tail positions in `body-ast` exactly as v1's `mark-tail!` did (application and macro-expand nodes in tail position of the body, recursing through `:if` branches and immediately-applied lambdas) so tail calls in macro-generated bodies still get the walker's TCO |\n   228\t| `yin/make-def name-ast value-ast` | `(yin/def <sym> value)` application; `name-ast` may be `:variable`, a `:literal` symbol, or a `:literal` string (§2.6 on why all three) |\n   229\t| `yin/name-of ast` | the symbol named by any of those three shapes; error otherwise |\n   230\t| `yin/macro-call op-ast operands` | constructs a `:yin/macro-expand` node — how a macro emits a call to another (or the same) macro |\n   231\t| `yin/gensym-sym prefix` | a fresh symbol `prefix__<E>_<n>` where `E` is the absolute value of the expansion-event eid and `n` an invocation-local counter; unique across expansions without cross-invocation state |\n   232\t\n   233\tPlus the ordinary `yin.vm/primitives` (arithmetic, `first`, `rest`,\n   234\t`conj`, `assoc`, `get`, `vec`, `=`, `nil?`, `empty?`) so a macro can walk and\n   235\tbuild vectors. `yin/def` inside a macro body writes to the throwaway store\n   236\tand is discarded; `require` resolves against the throwaway VM's `:modules`,\n   237\twhich is `nil` — a macro body that needs a module is an error, by design.\n   238\t\n   239\tThe throwaway VM is built with `:make-stream` absent and no FFI pair: a\n   240\tmacro body that reaches a stream or FFI effect fails with the existing\n   241\t\"constructed without :make-stream\" / \"no call pair\" errors. A macro body that\n   242\tparks (`:vm/park`) or blocks is an expansion error `{:kind :suspended}`.\n   243\tMacros are total, synchronous transformations of syntax; anything else is a\n   244\tfunction.\n   245\t\n   246\t**Hygiene** is v1's: non-hygienic by default, `yin/gensym-sym` on request.\n   247\tFull hygiene stays out of scope.\n   248\t\n   249\t### 2.5 `yang.clojure`\n   250\t\n   251\tAlready emits the representation above; three changes:\n   252\t\n   253\t1. `compile-program` gains a third arity taking an initial `macro-env`\n   254\t   (default `initial-macro-env`), so a caller that has macros from earlier\n   255\t   units (the REPL across evaluations, a build across files) can seed it.\n   256\t   `compile-program` also *returns* the final macro-env when asked\n   257\t   (`compile-program* forms tail? macro-env → {:ast :macro-env}`), so the\n   258\t   caller can persist it. The existing arities are unchanged.\n   259\t2. Non-top-level `(defmacro ...)` goes through the bootstrap `defmacro`\n   260\t   macro (`:yin/macro-expand` with operator `{:variable defmacro}` and\n   261\t   operands `[name params body*]`), and the expander's bootstrap table\n   262\t   expands it to `(yin/def name <lambda macro? true>)` — v1's `defmacro-fn`\n   263\t   over AST maps instead of eids. Top-level `defmacro` stays lowered directly\n   264\t   as today. A `defmacro` whose name symbol carries `^{:phase-policy\n   265\t   :runtime}` (or `:both`) metadata sets the lambda's policy; the default\n   266\t   stays `:compile`.\n   267\t3. `stdlib-forms` (the `defn` macro) moves from the deleted `yin.vm.macro`\n   268\t   to `yin.vm.macro/stdlib-forms`, unchanged in text; yang's docstring\n   269\t   already points there by name.\n   270\t\n   271\t### 2.6 Non-homoiconic frontends (`yang.python`, `yang.php`)\n   272\t\n   273\t`compile` gains an arity `(compile source macro-env)` where `macro-env` is a\n   274\tmap `symbol → {:type :user :eid e :lambda-ast ast}` (the same shape yang.clojure\n   275\tthreads). In `compile-stmt`, a `:call` whose function is a `:variable` naming\n   276\ta key of `macro-env` emits\n   277\t\n   278\t```\n   279\t{:type :yin/macro-expand\n   280\t :operator {:type :variable :name sym}\n   281\t :operands (mapv #(compile-stmt % false) args)}\n   282\t```\n   283\t\n   284\tinstead of `:application`. Names shadowed by an enclosing `lambda`/`def`\n   285\tparameter are removed from the env for that scope, as yang.clojure does.\n   286\t\n   287\t**Normalization is what the frontend already does.** `compile-stmt` on each\n   288\targument produces a canonical node: a Python identifier is a `:variable`, a\n   289\tstring is a `:literal` string, a number a `:literal`, a lambda a `:lambda`.\n   290\t`docs/cross-language-macro.md` §3 proposed that the frontend additionally\n   291\tlower `\"my_func\"` to a `:variable` node for `defn`'s benefit. This design\n   292\trejects that: a frontend must not know a macro's signature (that is a hidden\n   293\tcoupling from syntax to a particular macro), and it cannot know it for a\n   294\tmacro defined in another language. Instead the prelude's `yin/make-def` and\n   295\t`yin/name-of` accept a `:variable`, a `:literal` symbol, or a `:literal`\n   296\tstring. A Python `defn(\"my_func\", ...)` and a Clojure `(defn my-func ...)`\n   297\ttherefore reach the same macro with different canonical shapes, and the macro\n   298\tis written to accept both — which is a one-line accessor, not a frontend\n   299\trule. The contract for macro authors: *arguments arrive as whatever canonical\n   300\tnode the caller's frontend produces for that source form; use the prelude\n   301\taccessors, not `:type` pattern matching, for names.*\n   302\t\n   303\tPython and PHP remain *consumers* of macros in this design. Authoring from\n   304\tthose languages (`docs/cross-language-macro.md` §2, the direct AST API) is\n   305\tthe prelude itself — a Python program can call `yin.make_lambda(...)` once a\n   306\tPython `defmacro` surface exists — and is reserved; nothing here prevents it.\n   307\t\n   308\t### 2.7 Discovery: local and cross-unit\n   309\t\n   310\tA frontend needs to know which names are macros *before* emitting. Two\n   311\tsources, one shape:\n   312\t\n   313\t- **Same-unit (local).** yang.clojure's threaded `macro-env` today; the\n   314\t  Python/PHP frontends have no macro-defining form, so their local env is\n   315\t  always what they were given.\n   316\t- **Cross-unit.** The composition supplies `macro-env`. Where it comes from\n   317\t  is the composition's business: the REPL builds it by scanning each\n   318\t  evaluated batch (`macro/definitions datoms → {sym {:eid :lambda-ast\n   319\t  :phase-policy}}`, which finds `(yin/def sym <lambda macro? true>)`\n   320\t  applications, last-write-wins in batch order, exactly v1's\n   321\t  `find-macro-lambda-by-name` inverted into a map); a build tool builds it\n   322\t  from previously committed streams; DaoDB, when it can answer\n   323\t  `[?e :yin/macro? true]` joined to its `yin/def`, is a third producer of the\n   324\t  same map. No frontend queries anything itself.\n   325\t\n   326\tThe expander resolves a `:variable` operator by the same env plus the batch\n   327\tscan, in this order: batch definitions (same-unit wins) → supplied\n   328\t`:macro-env` → bootstrap table (`defmacro`) → error `{:kind :unresolved\n   329\t:name sym :call eid}`. At runtime (§4) the store replaces the first two.\n   330\t\n   331\t---\n   332\t\n   333\t## §3. The compile-time expander (`yin.vm.macro`)\n   334\t\n   335\t### 3.1 Contract\n   336\t\n   337\t```\n   338\texpand-batch : datoms opts → {:datoms datoms' :root-eid r :events [eid ...] :deferred [eid ...]}\n   339\texpand       : datoms opts → datoms'                    ; = (:datoms (expand-batch ...))\n   340\tdefinitions  : datoms → {sym {:eid :lambda-ast :phase-policy}}\n   341\tinvoke       : macro-ast operand-asts ctx → ast          ; one macro body, §3.2\n   342\tvalid-ast?   : value → nil | {:kind :invalid-output :at path}\n   343\tstdlib-forms : the defn macro source\n   344\tbootstrap    : {'defmacro (fn [operand-asts ctx] → ast)}\n   345\t```\n   346\t\n   347\t`opts`: `:macro-eval` (required — the composition supplies it; see §3.2),\n   348\t`:macro-env` (default `{}`), `:bootstrap` (default `bootstrap`), `:phase`\n   349\t(default `:compile`), `:max-depth` 100, `:max-datoms` 10000, `:t` (the\n   350\ttransaction slot written on emitted datoms, default 0), `:authorize`\n   351\t(default `(constantly true)` at compile time — §5.3).\n   352\t\n   353\t`datoms'` is `datoms` followed by the appended event and output datoms; the\n   354\tinput vector is a prefix of the output, byte for byte. Pure: same inputs,\n   355\tsame output, on every host.\n   356\t\n   357\t### 3.2 Executing the macro lambda\n   358\t\n   359\t`invoke` builds `env = (bind-params params operand-asts)` and calls\n   360\t\n   361\t```\n   362\t(:macro-eval opts) lambda-ast env prelude → value\n   363\t```\n   364\t\n   365\twhere the composition's `:macro-eval` is, for every current composition,\n   366\t`yin.vm.ast-walker/macro-eval`:\n   367\t\n   368\t```\n   369\t(defn macro-eval [lambda-ast env prelude]\n   370\t  (let [vm (create-vm {:env env\n   371\t                       :primitives (merge vm/primitives prelude)})\n   372\t        result (vm/eval vm (:body lambda-ast))]\n   373\t    (cond (vm/blocked? result) (throw (ex-info \"Macro body suspended\" {...}))\n   374\t          (not (vm/halted? result)) (throw ...)\n   375\t          :else (vm/value result))))\n   376\t```\n   377\t\n   378\t`yin.vm.macro` does **not** require `yin.vm.ast-walker`; the walker\n   379\trequires `macro` (for its runtime branch) and exports `macro-eval` for\n   380\tcompositions to hand back in. This breaks the require cycle and keeps the\n   381\tchoice of body evaluator where every other evaluator choice lives — the\n   382\tcomposition (`yin.repl.core/make-vm`, test utilities, build tools). A\n   383\tcomposition may supply any function of the same signature; the semantic VM\n   384\tis *not* an acceptable default for it (decision 2), but a host that ports\n   385\tonly a walker can still expand macros.\n   386\t\n   387\tThe throwaway VM is constructed per invocation. Construction cost (a record\n   388\tand a map) is negligible next to running a body, and per-invocation\n   389\tconstruction is what makes the \"no shared mutable state\" row of §1.2 true\n   390\twithout argument.\n   391\t\n   392\t### 3.3 The algorithm\n   393\t\n   394\tPost-order over the *tree reachable from the root*, with outermost-first\n   395\texpansion at each macro node and immediate re-expansion of the result:\n   396\t\n   397\t```\n   398\texpand-node ctx eid :\n   399\t  node-type = type(eid)\n   400\t  if node-type = :yin/macro-expand and enabled?(ctx, macro(eid)):\n   401\t      guard depth\n   402\t      operands  = [reconstruct ctx o | o ∈ operands(eid)]      ; AST maps with :eid kept\n   403\t      macro     = resolve ctx operator(eid)                    ; §2.7\n   404\t      event-eid = fresh ctx\n   405\t      out-ast   = invoke macro operands ctx'                   ; ctx' carries event-eid for gensym\n   406\t      check valid-ast? out-ast\n   407\t      [root', ctx''] = emit ctx' out-ast m=event-eid           ; §3.5\n   408\t      ctx''' = emit-event ctx'' event-eid eid macro root'\n   409\t      guard datoms\n   410\t      return expand-node (ctx''' with depth+1) root'           ; re-expand the output\n   411\t  else:\n   412\t      for each ref attribute a of eid, child c:\n   413\t          [c', ctx] = expand-node ctx c\n   414\t      if any c' ≠ c: copy eid to fresh eid' with substituted refs, m = default-op\n   415\t      return [eid or eid', ctx]\n   416\t```\n   417\t\n   418\tProperties, each of which is a test in Phase 1:\n   419\t\n   420\t- **Fixpoint by construction.** Every node reachable from the final root has\n   421\t  been visited after its last rewrite; a `:yin/macro-expand` remains only if\n   422\t  `enabled?` was false for it (a `:runtime`-policy macro at compile time),\n   423\t  and each such node is listed in `:deferred`. A final scan of the output\n   424\t  tree asserts this and is cheap (linear in the output).\n   425\t- **Outermost first.** `operands` are reconstructed *unexpanded*. A macro\n   426\t  such as `->` or `when-let` that rewrites forms containing other macro calls\n   427\t  sees them as written. This differs from v1's innermost-first `transform`\n   428\t  (Appendix A.2).\n   429\t- **Depth** counts nested re-expansion: a macro whose output contains a macro\n   430\t  call whose output contains a macro call ... is depth 3. Direct recursion\n   431\t  (`(defmacro loop* ... (yin/macro-call loop* ...))`) without a base case\n   432\t  hits the guard at 100 with `{:kind :depth-guard :depth 100 :chain [eid ...]}`\n   433\t  — the chain of call eids is in the error, so the offending macro is\n   434\t  nameable from the error alone. Sibling expansions do not accumulate depth.\n   435\t- **Sharing.** An operand that a macro returns unchanged (the same map, still\n   436\t  carrying its `:eid`) is referenced, not copied (§3.5). The typical `defn`\n   437\t  expansion therefore emits the new `yin/def` application and lambda nodes\n   438\t  and *refs* the body the user wrote; only the ancestor chain from the call\n   439\t  site up to the root is copied, as in v1.\n   440\t- **Phase.** `enabled?` is: `:compile` or `:both` when `(:phase opts)` is\n   441\t  `:compile`; `:runtime` or `:both` when it is `:runtime` (the runtime callers\n   442\t  in §4 use the same function with `:phase :runtime` and a single node). A\n   443\t  `:compile` macro encountered with `:phase :runtime` is a hard error\n   444\t  `{:kind :phase-violation}`: it means the compile pass was skipped, and\n   445\t  silently expanding it at runtime would hide that.\n   446\t\n   447\t### 3.4 Guards and failure\n   448\t\n   449\t| Guard | Default | Error data |\n   450\t|---|---|---|\n   451\t| depth | 100 | `{:kind :depth-guard :depth d :chain [call-eid ...]}` |\n   452\t| emitted datoms per `expand-batch` call | 10,000 | `{:kind :datom-guard :emitted n :call call-eid}` |\n   453\t| arity | — | `{:kind :arity :macro eid :expected :got}` |\n   454\t| output shape | — | `{:kind :invalid-output :macro eid :at [path]}` |\n   455\t| unresolved macro | — | `{:kind :unresolved :name sym :call eid}` |\n   456\t| phase | — | `{:kind :phase-violation :macro eid :policy p :phase ph}` |\n   457\t| suspended body | — | `{:kind :suspended :macro eid}` |\n   458\t| authority | — | `{:kind :unauthorized :macro eid :phase ph}` |\n   459\t\n   460\tEvery failure is thrown as `ex-info` whose data is the map above under\n   461\t`:yin/error`, **and** — when an event eid was already minted — the expander\n   462\tfirst appends the event entity with `:yin/error <that map>` and no\n   463\t`:yin/expansion-root`, so a failed expansion is queryable (macro-design.md\n   464\tlists `:yin/error` as an event attribute for this reason). `expand-batch`\n   465\titself does not return partial output: the throw carries `:datoms-so-far`\n   466\tfor diagnosis only.\n   467\t\n   468\t### 3.5 Output, the root fact, and eid discipline\n   469\t\n   470\t**Emission.** `emit` is `vm/ast->datoms-with-root` with three options that\n   471\tPhase 0 adds to `yin.vm`:\n   472\t\n   473\t- `:m event-eid` — already supported; every output datom carries it.\n   474\t- `:id-start` — already supported; the context's next fresh eid, below the\n   475\t  batch minimum (v1's `make-eid-counter!` rule: strictly below every eid in\n   476\t  the input so tempids never collide).\n   477\t- `:existing #{eid ...}` — **new**: a node carrying `:eid` in this set is\n   478\t  returned as a reference and *not* re-emitted. This is the sharing rule.\n   479\t  `datoms->ast` gains the inverse option `:keep-eids? true` and the expander\n   480\t  reconstructs operands with it. `vm-load-program` uses `:keep-eids?` only\n   481\t  for macro lambdas (so closures can carry `:eid`, §2.3) — ordinary nodes stay\n   482\t  eid-free on the walker's hot path and in the `compile` command's output.\n   483\t\n   484\t**The root fact.** After the top-level `expand-node` returns `root'`, the\n   485\texpander appends `[root' :yin/root true t default-op]` (retracting nothing —\n   486\tan older `:yin/root` on the original root, if a previous pass wrote one, is\n   487\tsuperseded by \"last `:yin/root` fact in batch order wins\"). `index-datoms`\n   488\tgains: if any `:yin/root` fact exists, the root is the entity of the last one;\n   489\totherwise the existing heuristic, restricted to entities whose `:yin/type` is\n   490\tnot `:macro-expand-event`. Both are one-line changes in `yin.vm` and the\n   491\tsecond is required even for batches with no `:yin/root`, because an event\n   492\tentity is typed and unreferenced and would otherwise win the heuristic.\n   493\t`:yin/root` joins `schema` as a boolean.\n   494\t\n   495\t**Eid discipline across batches.** Within one `expand-batch` call, all refs\n   496\tare within one batch and valid as tempids. A macro resolved through\n   497\t`:macro-env` from another unit has an eid that belongs to that unit's batch;\n   498\tthe event's `:yin/macro` ref is then meaningful only once both batches are\n   499\tcommitted to the same DaoDB with tempids resolved — the pipeline\n   500\t`docs/cross-language-macro.md` §4 describes. The expander records the fact\n   501\thonestly and also writes `:yin/macro-name sym` on the event, so the link\n   502\tis recoverable by name even when the ref is cross-batch. `:yin/macro-name`\n   503\tis the only attribute this design adds to the event beyond macro-design.md.\n   504\t\n   505\t### 3.6 Feeding the three evaluators\n   506\t\n   507\tThe output of `expand-batch` is a batch of `:yin/*` datoms with an explicit\n   508\troot and no compile-enabled macro nodes. Each consumer takes it unchanged:\n   509\t\n   510\t1. **`yin.vm.ast-walker`**: `vm-load-program` calls `datoms->ast`, which\n   511\t   now honours `:yin/root`; the walker never sees a `:yin/macro-expand`\n   512\t   node unless the batch carried a `:runtime`/`:both` macro that was\n   513\t   deferred — and then it takes the §4.1 transition. Composition:\n   514\t   `(comp ast-walker/vm-load-program (macro/expand-with opts))` handed to\n   515\t   `observer/run-on-stream`, or `macro/expand` applied to `(vm/ast->datoms\n   516\t   ast)` before `vm-load-program` in a direct evaluation path.\n   517\t2. **`yin.vm.linearize` → `yin.vm.semantic`**: `lower` reads the same\n   518\t   root fact; its existing rejection of `:yin/macro-expand` becomes the\n   519\t   §4.2 rule (reject `:compile`-policy nodes with `:phase-violation`, emit\n   520\t   `:macro-call` for the rest). Composition: `(comp semantic/vm-load-program\n   521\t   linearize/lower (macro/expand-with opts))`.\n   522\t3. **`yin.vm.stack`** (future): the bytecode compiler consumes the same\n   523\t   batch through `datoms->ast` or the datom index; deferred nodes become\n   524\t   `OP_MACRO_EXPAND` (§4.3).\n   525\t\n   526\t\"Zero runtime overhead\" is literal: after the pass, no evaluator has a macro\n   527\tnode on its path, and the walker's inlined hot loop (`ast-walker-run-active-continuation`)\n   528\tdoes not gain a case — the `:yin/macro-expand` branch lives in\n   529\t`cesk-transition`, which the hot loop reaches only through its default arm.\n   530\t\n   531\t### 3.7 As a stream interpreter\n   532\t\n   533\t`expand-with opts` returns a unary `batch → batch'` for loader composition.\n   534\tFor a build pipeline that keeps expansion out of the loader, `macro/forward-step`\n   535\tis one step of a forwarder: read one batch at a cursor on a program-in\n   536\thandle, `expand-batch`, `append!` to a program-out handle, return the\n   537\tsuccessor cursor and the append outcome. It is exactly the forwarder shape\n   538\t`dao.stream.md` §Composition describes (\"a single step, not a callback\n   539\tloop\"); cadence is the driver's; a `full` on program-out retains the batch\n   540\tand returns `:retry`. This is optional composition, not part of the\n   541\tevaluators, and is listed so that \"compile-time expansion as a\n   542\tstream-transducer pass\" has one concrete function to point at.\n   543\t\n   544\t---\n   545\t\n   546\t## §4. Runtime macro execution across evaluators\n   547\t\n   548\tA runtime expansion is `expand-node` from §3.3 applied to a single call node\n   549\twith `:phase :runtime`, where *resolve* consults the VM's store instead of the\n   550\tbatch, *invoke* is the same, *authorize* is the VM's `:macro-authorize`, and\n   551\t*emit* targets the VM's ledger rather than the batch. The evaluator-specific\n   552\tpart is only how the result is spliced into control.\n   553\t\n   554\t### 4.1 `yin.vm.ast-walker`\n   555\t\n   556\t**Loader.** `vm-load-program` records `(:min-eid batch)` on the VM (one\n   557\t`reduce` over the batch, done inside the existing `datoms->ast` index pass)\n   558\tso runtime-minted eids continue strictly below the loaded program's.\n   559\t\n   560\t**Transition.** A new arm in `cesk-transition`'s node `case`\n   561\t(`ast_walker.cljc:390-500`), not in the inlined hot loop:\n   562\t\n   563\t```\n   564\t:yin/macro-expand\n   565\t(let [{:keys [operator operands]} node\n   566\t      macro   (resolve-macro state operator env)          ; (a)\n   567\t      _       (check-phase macro :runtime)                 ; (b)\n   568\t      _       (authorize state macro)                      ; (c)\n   569\t      {:keys [ast events state']} (macro/expand-runtime state macro operands node) ; (d)\n   570\t  (cesk-return state' ast env k nil))                      ; (e)\n   571\t```\n   572\t\n   573\t(a) `resolve-macro`: an operator node of type `:lambda` with `:macro?` is\n   574\tbuilt into a closure as the `:lambda` arm would (its `:eid` carried); an\n   575\toperator of type `:variable` is `engine/resolve-var` on `env store primitives\n   576\tmodules`, and the result must be a closure with `:macro?` (a function or\n   577\tprimitive here is `{:kind :not-a-macro}`); anything else is `{:kind\n   578\t:unresolved}`.\n   579\t\n   580\t(b) `:phase-policy` must be `:runtime` or `:both`; `:compile` is\n   581\t`{:kind :phase-violation}` — the compile pass was skipped (§3.3).\n   582\t\n   583\t(c) §5.3.\n   584\t\n   585\t(d) `macro/expand-runtime` runs §3.3's `expand-node` on the single node with\n   586\toperands taken *as AST maps directly from the node* — they are already maps\n   587\tin the walker, no reconstruction — and `:phase :runtime`, minting eids from\n   588\t`(:macro-eid state)` (initialised to `(dec (:min-eid state))` at load) and\n   589\tthreading the counter back. It returns the expanded AST map, the datoms for\n   590\tthe event and the output (with `m` = event eid), and the state with\n   591\t`:macro-expansions` extended by `{:event event-eid :datoms [...]}` and the\n   592\tcounter advanced. The output AST is macro-free for `:runtime`: nested\n   593\truntime macro calls in the output were expanded recursively under the same\n   594\tdepth guard; nested `:compile`-policy calls in the output are\n   595\t`{:kind :phase-violation}` (a runtime macro may not manufacture compile-time\n   596\twork).\n   597\t\n   598\t(e) The expanded AST becomes control with the same `env` and `k`. The\n   599\toperands were never evaluated: what the brief calls \"evaluating unevaluated\n   600\tAST operands\" is, precisely, the identity — the operand nodes are bound as\n   601\tdata, per macro-design.md \"Argument semantics\". `val` is `nil` because the\n   602\texpansion produced no value; the expansion's value is whatever the spliced\n   603\tAST computes. Tail position needs no treatment: the spliced AST runs against\n   604\tthe caller's `k`, which is how the walker already gets TCO.\n   605\t\n   606\tCESK, with $M$ the ledger and $\\mu$ the eid counter (both fields of the state\n   607\trecord, not of $S$):\n   608\t\n   609\t$$\\langle \\text{macro-expand}(op, args), E, S, K, M, \\mu\\rangle\n   610\t\\to \\langle \\mathcal{E}\\llbracket m \\rrbracket(args), E, S, K, M \\Vert [\\text{ev}], \\mu' \\rangle\n   611\t\\quad \\text{where } m = \\rho_{macro}(op, E, S)$$\n   612\t\n   613\tand $\\mathcal{E}$ is the expander; the transition is a hard error if\n   614\t$\\rho_{macro}$ fails, the phase or authority check fails, or $\\mathcal{E}$\n   615\traises. Exactly one `ASTWalkerVM` allocation, as every other arm.\n   616\t\n   617\t**Blocked and parked states** are unaffected: expansion is synchronous\n   618\tinside one transition; the throwaway VM's blocking is an error (§2.4), never\n   619\ta park of the outer VM.\n   620\t\n   621\t**`vm-eval` without a compile pass.** `eval` converts, loads and runs; it does\n   622\t*not* expand. A `:compile`-policy macro node therefore reaches (b) and fails\n   623\twith `:phase-violation`. This is deliberate: `eval` is \"direct evaluation of\n   624\tsupplied work\", and a composition that wants compile-time macros on that path\n   625\tcomposes `macro/expand` before it (the REPL does, §6.2).\n   626\t\n   627\t### 4.2 `yin.vm.semantic` (linear code segments)\n   628\t\n   629\t**Representation.** One new instruction and three operand attributes:\n   630\t\n   631\t| `:yin.code/op` | Operand attributes | Effect |\n   632\t|---|---|---|\n   633\t| `:macro-call` | `:yin.code/macro-ref ref` **or** `:yin.code/name sym`; `:yin.code/call-ast <map>`; `:yin.code/tail? bool`; `:yin.code/source ref` | expand and call the ephemeral segment (below) |\n   634\t| `:closure` (extended) | `:yin.code/macro? true`, `:yin.code/phase-policy kw`, `:yin.code/macro-ast <map>` — on macro lambdas only | `val ← closure with :macro? :phase-policy :macro-ast :eid` |\n   635\t\n   636\t`:yin.code/call-ast` is the `:yin/macro-expand` node *as an AST map value*\n   637\t(operator and operands inline), and `:yin.code/macro-ast` is the macro\n   638\tlambda as a map. These are inlined as values rather than left as refs because\n   639\ta segment travels alone as one batch and a continuation names its segment by\n   640\tid (semantic spec §2.2, §7.3): the segment must be self-contained, and the\n   641\texecutor needs *syntax* at these two points because the instruction's meaning\n   642\tis \"interpret this syntax\". They are also the only two places the code\n   643\tvocabulary holds AST, and both are absent from any segment lowered from a\n   644\tmacro-free batch. Opcode: `:macro-call 24` in `opcode-table` and in\n   645\t`opcase`'s literal map (the cljd trap, semantic spec Appendix B).\n   646\t\n   647\t**Lowering rule** (`linearize`): a `:yin/macro-expand` node with policy\n   648\t`:compile` (resolved through the batch and the supplied `:macro-env`) is a\n   649\tlowering error `{:kind :phase-violation}`; with `:runtime`/`:both` it lowers\n   650\tto one `:macro-call` whose `:yin.code/macro-ref` is the lambda entity when the\n   651\toperator is an embedded lambda, or `:yin.code/name` when it is a variable.\n   652\tTail position is `:yin/tail?` as for `:call`. The lowered *segment* for a\n   653\tprogram with deferred macros is otherwise ordinary.\n   654\t\n   655\t**Loader.** Builds `:macro-sites {pc {:call-ast :macro-ref/:name :tail?}}`\n   656\tbeside the image; the decoded instruction holds only the pc. Macro-lambda\n   657\tclosure instructions decode their three extra fields.\n   658\t\n   659\t**Dynamic lowering and splice.** On `:macro-call` at `(seg, pc)`:\n   660\t\n   661\t1. Resolve: `:yin.code/macro-ref` → the closure value produced by that\n   662\t   lambda's `:closure` instruction is not available by ref at run time (a\n   663\t   closure is a value made when the instruction executes), so the loader\n   664\t   resolves the ref to the *instruction* and the executor reads its\n   665\t   `:macro-ast` directly — a macro named by entity is static. `:yin.code/name`\n   666\t   → `engine/resolve-var`; must be a closure with `:macro?` (its\n   667\t   `:macro-ast` is the lambda).\n   668\t2. Check phase and authority as in §4.1 (b), (c).\n   669\t3. `macro/expand-runtime` with the operands from `:call-ast`, `:phase\n   670\t   :runtime`, eids below `(:min-eid vm)` (recorded by the loader as for the\n   671\t   walker), producing `ast'`, the ledger entry, and the counter.\n   672\t4. `linearize/lower-ast ast' {:terminator :return :derived-from root' :m\n   673\t   event-eid}` → an **ephemeral segment** $\\sigma$: a fresh segment entity\n   674\t   whose main sequence ends in `:return` instead of `:halt`, whose\n   675\t   instructions carry `:yin.code/source` refs into the expansion output and\n   676\t   `m` = the event eid, and whose datoms are appended to the ledger entry\n   677\t   (so the ledger holds both the AST-level and the code-level record of the\n   678\t   expansion, and a query can render either view).\n   679\t5. Load $\\sigma$ into `:code` under its id (never evicted in Phase 3; if\n   680\t   eviction is added it pins by frames as the semantic spec's Appendix B\n   681\t   says).\n   682\t6. Splice by *segment call*, which binds nothing:\n   683\t\n   684\t$$\\begin{aligned}\n   685\t&\\neg tail:&& \\langle seg,pc,val,St,E,S,K\\rangle \\to \\langle \\sigma,\\,0,\\,val,\\,St,\\,E,\\,S,\\,K \\Vert [\\mathrm{ret}(seg,pc{+}1,E,|St|)]\\rangle\\\\\n   686\t&tail:&& \\to \\langle \\sigma,\\,0,\\,val,\\,St,\\,E,\\,S,\\,K\\rangle\n   687\t\\end{aligned}$$\n   688\t\n   689\tThe `:return` at the end of $\\sigma$ pops the frame and continues at\n   690\t`pc+1` with `val` = the expansion's result, exactly as a closure return\n   691\twould; in tail position no frame is pushed, so a runtime macro in tail\n   692\tposition of a loop does not grow $K$ — the same rule as `tailcall`. The env\n   693\tis the *caller's* $E$, unchanged, because the expanded code is the caller's\n   694\tcode; it is not a closure body. `St` is untouched: the linearizer emits the\n   695\t`:macro-call` where the walker would have had the node, i.e. in value\n   696\tposition, and the expansion's value lands in `val` where the enclosing\n   697\tsequence expects it.\n   698\t\n   699\tThe image of $\\sigma$ is built once per expansion. A `:macro-call` in a loop\n   700\tbody re-expands on every iteration; a memo keyed by `[pc call-eid]` is\n   701\t*reserved* (Appendix B), not default, because a runtime macro may read the\n   702\tstore and must be allowed to see it change.\n   703\t\n   704\t### 4.3 `yin.vm.stack` (future bytecode VM)\n   705\t\n   706\tPer the semantic spec §6.4 the loaded segment image *is* bytecode; a stack\n   707\tVM differs in stored form only. The contract for it, so that its designer\n   708\tinherits this document rather than re-deriving it:\n   709\t\n   710\t- `OP_MACRO_EXPAND` = 24 (shared `opcode-table`), operand = an index into the\n   711\t  code object's constant pool holding the `:yin/macro-expand` AST map and the\n   712\t  resolution key (`{:macro-ref e}` or `{:name sym}`), plus a tail flag.\n   713\t- Execution: resolve, check phase and authority, `macro/expand-runtime`,\n   714\t  compile `ast'` to an ephemeral code object with a `RETURN` terminator,\n   715\t  push a return frame unless tail, jump. Identical to §4.2 with \"segment\"\n   716\t  read as \"code object\".\n   717\t- Compile-time: none needed; the compiler consumes the expanded batch (§3.6).\n   718\t- Parity: a corpus program with a runtime macro must produce the same value,\n   719\t  effect order and ledger events on all three evaluators; the walker is the\n   720\t  oracle.\n   721\t\n   722\t### 4.4 Why the transition does not append to a stream\n   723\t\n   724\t`streams-all-the-way-down.md` §1 puts a boundary \"wherever you need\n   725\tdecoupling — and nowhere else\". An expansion record has one consumer (the\n   726\tcomposition that persists or displays it), needs no replay inside the VM,\n   727\tand crosses no host boundary at the moment it is produced. Appending it from\n   728\tinside the transition would put a stream handle in the executor's path and\n   729\tforce the transition to be total over `full` (park the *expansion*?) for a\n   730\trecord that is not the program's own effect. The ledger is a field on the VM\n   731\tvalue — like `:parked`, `:wait-set`, and the reserved trace vocabulary of the\n   732\tsemantic spec §3.6 — and `macro/drain-expansions vm → [vm' datoms]` hands the\n   733\tcomposition one batch to append wherever it likes. A composition that wants\n   734\tthe expansion log on a stream composes that in one step, as the REPL composes\n   735\toutput. This is the same decision, for the same reason, as the semantic spec\n   736\tmakes for traces.\n   737\t\n   738\t---\n   739\t\n   740\t## §5. Provenance, causality and capability security\n   741\t\n   742\t### 5.1 Non-destructive expansion\n   743\t\n   744\tOriginal call datoms are never rewritten. At compile time the input vector\n   745\tis a prefix of the output; at runtime the program the loader received is\n   746\tuntouched and the ledger holds only appended facts. The *link* from an\n   747\toriginal call to its expansion is forward-only: `event.:yin/source-call →\n   748\tcall`, never a fact on the call itself. The expanded program is a new root\n   749\tthat references unchanged subtrees; the ancestor chain from a call site to\n   750\tthe root is copied with fresh eids and `m = default-op` (they are not macro\n   751\toutput; they are re-parenting, and their provenance is structural).\n   752\t\n   753\t### 5.2 The expansion event\n   754\t\n   755\t```\n   756\t[ev :yin/type           :macro-expand-event  t default-op]\n   757\t[ev :yin/source-call    <call-eid>           t default-op]\n   758\t[ev :yin/macro          <macro-lambda-eid>   t default-op]   ; omitted for bootstrap macros (no entity)\n   759\t[ev :yin/macro-name     <sym>                t default-op]   ; always present\n   760\t[ev :yin/phase          :compile | :runtime  t default-op]\n   761\t[ev :yin/expansion-root <root'>              t default-op]   ; omitted on failure\n   762\t[ev :yin/error          {:kind ...}          t default-op]   ; on failure only\n   763\t[ev :yin/capability     <token-ref>          t default-op]   ; runtime, when a token was presented\n   764\t[ev :yin/timestamp      <logical-t>          t default-op]   ; optional, §5.2.1\n   765\t```\n   766\t\n   767\tand every output datom (AST nodes, and for the semantic VM the ephemeral\n   768\tsegment's instructions) carries `m = ev`. The event's own datoms carry\n   769\t`default-op` in `m`, as v1's did: an event is not the output of itself.\n   770\t\n   771\tThe query chain macro-design.md requires — source-call → event →\n   772\texpansion-root — is three joins on refs. \"Every datom this macro ever\n   773\tproduced\" is `[?ev :yin/macro ?m] [?d _ _ _ ?ev]`; \"what was here before\n   774\texpansion\" is the subtree under `:yin/source-call`.\n   775\t\n   776\t**5.2.1 `:yin/timestamp`.** The brief lists it; macro-design.md does not.\n   777\tIt is included as an *optional logical* value: the `:t` the caller passed\n   778\tto `expand-batch` (or the VM's step counter at runtime, once the trace\n   779\tvocabulary of the semantic spec §3.6 exists). It is never a host clock.\n   780\tReading a clock inside the expander would make expansion non-deterministic\n   781\tand the \"compile/runtime parity for the same macro and input\" test of\n   782\tmacro-design.md unrunnable. A composition that wants wall time writes it on\n   783\tthe batch it commits.\n   784\t\n   785\t### 5.3 Authority\n   786\t\n   787\tThe check is a supplied function, so that the Shibi design (macaroon-style\n   788\tattenuable tokens, `dao.space.security.md`, ADR-0002) can land without\n   789\treopening this file:\n   790\t\n   791\t```\n   792\t:macro-authorize : (fn [{:keys [phase macro-eid macro-name program-root capability]}]\n   793\t                     → true | {:kind :unauthorized ...})\n   794\t```\n   795\t\n   796\t- **Compile time** (`expand-batch`): `:authorize` defaults to\n   797\t  `(constantly true)` — the build context is trusted, per macro-design.md.\n   798\t  A composition may pass a stricter one (a build that only permits macros\n   799\t  from a namespace allowlist).\n   800\t- **Runtime** (walker, semantic, stack): `:macro-authorize` is a `create-vm`\n   801\t  option. **No default.** A VM constructed without one denies every runtime\n   802\t  expansion with `{:kind :unauthorized :reason :no-authorizer}`. The\n   803\t  `:capability` it is handed is the value under store key `:yin/capability`\n   804\t  if the composition put one there (`create-vm` option `:capability`, stored\n   805\t  like the FFI pair), else `nil`. `program-root` is the loaded root eid\n   806\t  (`:program-root` recorded by the loader). The token, when present, is also\n   807\t  written on the event as `:yin/capability`, so every runtime expansion's\n   808\t  authority is auditable from the facts.\n   809\t- **What a Shibi authorizer will check** (not implemented, as macro-design.md\n   810\t  says): authority `macro/expand`, scope = the program root, phase\n   811\t  `:runtime`, and an allowed macro entity or namespace — each a caveat on\n   812\t  the token. The interface above carries exactly those four inputs and\n   813\t  nothing else, so the caveat set is closed.\n   814\t\n   815\tThe REPL supplies `(constantly true)` for the local shell (a user typing at\n   816\ttheir own shell is the trusted build context of §5.3 bullet one), and states\n   817\tso in `repl-state` under `:macros {:runtime-authorized? true}`.\n   818\t\n   819\t---\n   820\t\n   821\t## §6. Coexistence and migration\n   822\t\n   823\t### 6.1 The divergence register\n   824\t\n   825\t`docs/design/yin.vm.divergence-register.md` changes in two places (Phase\n   826\t0 writes the text; the phases that follow make it true):\n   827\t\n   828\t- \"The five user-visible changes\", item 2, becomes: *\"User-defined macros\n   829\t  are expanded by `yin.vm.macro`, not by an evaluator. `:compile`\n   830\t  (default) and `:both` policies are expanded by a batch pass composed into\n   831\t  the loader; `:runtime` and `:both` are expanded at a `:yin/macro-expand`\n   832\t  transition (walker) or `:macro-call` instruction (semantic) under a\n   833\t  supplied authorizer. v1's evaluator-embedded expansion (`semantic`,\n   834\t  `register`, `stack`, `space` each calling `yin.vm.macro`) has no v2\n   835\t  counterpart. Direct `eval` does not expand; the composition does.\"*\n   836\t- A new section **Macros** recording: expansion order is outermost-first\n   837\t  where v1 was innermost-first; macro arguments are AST maps where v1's were\n   838\t  eids with `get-attr`; the root is an explicit `:yin/root` fact; a macro\n   839\t  closure applied as a function is an error where v1 would have evaluated\n   840\t  its operands; runtime expansion requires an authorizer where v1 had a TODO;\n   841\t  bootstrap `defmacro` events carry `:yin/macro-name` and no `:yin/macro`.\n   842\t- The scope sentence \"`macro` … not ported\" is removed from the header.\n   843\t\n   844\t`parity_test.cljc`'s docstring (\"the corpus is macro-free because neither\n   845\tevaluator has a `macro-expand` branch\") is updated to say the corpus is\n   846\tmacro-free because v1's walker has none; the v2 macro corpus lives in its\n   847\town suites (§7).\n   848\t\n   849\t### 6.2 `yin.repl`\n   850\t\n   851\t- `make-vm` passes `:macro-eval ast-walker/macro-eval` and\n   852\t  `:macro-authorize (constantly true)` to the constructor.\n   853\t- The shell state gains `:macro-env {}`. After every successful evaluation\n   854\t  the shell merges `(macro/definitions expanded-batch)` into it. That env is\n   855\t  handed to `yang.clojure/compile-program` (new arity, §2.5) and to\n   856\t  `yang.python/compile` and `yang.php/compile` (§2.6), and to\n   857\t  `macro/expand-with` as `:macro-env`. `(reset)` and `(vm ...)` clear it with\n   858\t  the store, since the macro closures live in the store.\n   859\t- **One program path.** `eval-ast` stops calling `vm/eval` and instead\n   860\t  converts the AST to datoms and takes the `eval-datoms` path: append to the\n   861\t  program medium, `run-on-stream` with the loader\n   862\t  `(comp <evaluator-loader> (macro/expand-with {:macro-eval … :macro-env …}))`.\n   863\t  This is the change that makes compile-time expansion a loader composition\n   864\t  in the shell rather than a special case, and it removes the shell's second\n   865\t  evaluation path. `vm/eval` remains for direct callers and tests.\n   866\t- `(compile expr)` renders AST, datoms, and — new — the expanded datoms and\n   867\t  the events, so a user can see an expansion without running it.\n   868\t- `defmacro` at the prompt is just a form: top-level → yang lowers it to\n   869\t  `yin/def`; the batch runs; the store now holds a macro closure; the shell's\n   870\t  `:macro-env` learns the name from `definitions`. The next input that calls\n   871\t  it is compiled with that env and emits `:yin/macro-expand`.\n   872\t- `help-text` and `repl-state` mention macros; no other shell behaviour\n   873\t  changes.\n   874\t\n   875\t### 6.3 Cross-platform portability\n   876\t\n   877\t- **One `.cljc` per namespace**, no host branches in the expander: it is\n   878\t  maps, vectors and `reduce`.\n   879\t- **cljd**: the `opcase` literal map gains `:macro-call 24` alongside\n   880\t  `opcode-table` (semantic spec Appendix B); `catch` uses the\n   881\t  `#?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default)` form; no `^:unsynchronized-mutable`\n   882\t  fields — the ledger and counters are record fields updated by `assoc`; any\n   883\t  reader conditional excluding host code puts `:cljd` first (the\n   884\t  `#?(:clj …)` trap: it does not exclude code from the cljd host-eval pass).\n   885\t- **cljs**: keyword mnemonics are compared only in `linearize` and the\n   886\t  loader; the expander compares `:type` keywords with `=` on maps it built\n   887\t  itself; symbols from `yin/gensym-sym` are built with `symbol`, not string\n   888\t  identity.\n   889\t- **Test discovery**: shadow `:node-test` auto-discovers `*-test`\n   890\t  namespaces; each new suite's \"Testing yin.vm.macro-test\" line is\n   891\t  confirmed in the node output as part of acceptance.\n   892\t- The prelude's `yin/make-lambda` tail-marking and `yin/sequence-body` are\n   893\t  ported from the deleted v1 code as map operations; v1's datom-emitting\n   894\t  versions are not reused.\n   895\t\n   896\t---\n   897\t\n   898\t## §7. Phased implementation roadmap\n   899\t\n   900\t### Phase 0 — Spec and contract\n   901\t\n   902\tDeliverables:\n   903\t- `docs/design/macro-design.md` amended to v2: §2–§5 of this document\n   904\t  promoted (argument protocol, prelude table, outermost-first order, root\n   905\t  fact, event attributes including `:yin/macro-name`, authorizer interface);\n   906\t  the \"assumes register/stack\" sentence and `maybe-recompile-at-boundary`\n   907\t  runtime flow removed (the semantic VM's segments are immutable; runtime\n   908\t  splicing is §4.2, not recompilation).\n   909\t- `docs/cross-language-macro.md` status line changed from \"target deleted\"\n   910\t  to \"target is `yin.vm.macro/expand-batch`\", §3 normalization paragraph\n   911\t  replaced by §2.6's rule.\n   912\t- Divergence register text (§6.1).\n   913\t- `yin.vm`: `schema` gains `:yin/root` (boolean) and `:yin/macro-name`\n   914\t  (symbol); `index-datoms` honours `:yin/root` and excludes event entities\n   915\t  from the heuristic; `ast->datoms-with-root` gains `:existing`;\n   916\t  `datoms->ast` gains `:keep-eids?`; `opcode-table` and `opcase` gain\n   917\t  `:macro-call 24`.\n   918\t\n   919\tTests: `yin.vm-test` additions — root fact wins over heuristic; event\n   920\tentity never chosen as root; `:existing` yields a ref and no datoms;\n   921\t`:keep-eids?` round-trips `:eid`. Acceptance: all existing v2 suites green\n   922\ton three hosts; walker `compile` output unchanged (no `:eid` leaks).\n   923\t\n   924\t### Phase 1 — Compile-time expander\n   925\t\n   926\tDeliverables: `src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc` — `expand-batch`, `expand`,\n   927\t`expand-with`, `definitions`, `invoke`, `bind-params`, `valid-ast?`,\n   928\tprelude, `bootstrap` (`defmacro`), `stdlib-forms`, `forward-step`;\n   929\t`yin.vm.ast-walker/macro-eval` (the one walker change in this phase —\n   930\tadditive, exported, no transition touched).\n   931\t\n   932\tTests: `test/yin/vm/macro_test.cljc`:\n   933\t- `defn` from `stdlib-forms` expands a yang.clojure program; the walker runs\n   934\t  the expanded batch and the value matches the same program compiled with\n   935\t  yang's native `defn` fallback (parity of the two `defn` paths).\n   936\t- Provenance: event shape; every output datom's `m` is the event; the\n   937\t  original call datoms are present and unchanged (vector prefix equality);\n   938\t  the query chain source-call → event → root resolves.\n   939\t- Sharing: an operand returned unchanged is referenced, not copied (count\n   940\t  of output datoms equals the macro's own emission).\n   941\t- Outermost-first: a macro that inspects an operand containing a macro call\n   942\t  sees `:yin/macro-expand`.\n   943\t- Fixpoint: a macro whose output calls another macro yields a tree with no\n   944\t  enabled macro node; the final scan finds none.\n   945\t- Guards: direct recursion trips `:depth-guard` at 100 with the chain in the\n   946\t  error; a macro emitting a 10,001-node vector trips `:datom-guard`.\n   947\t- Phase: a `:runtime` macro is listed in `:deferred` and left intact; a\n   948\t  `:compile` macro under `:phase :runtime` fails `:phase-violation`.\n   949\t- Invalid output (a bare symbol) fails `:invalid-output` with a path; the\n   950\t  failed event with `:yin/error` is in the returned diagnostic datoms.\n   951\t- Arity and variadic `&` binding; `yin/gensym-sym` uniqueness across two\n   952\t  expansions in one batch; a body that calls `stream/make` fails with the\n   953\t  existing constructor error.\n   954\t- `definitions` finds a `yin/def` macro and prefers the last in batch order.\n   955\t- Bootstrap `defmacro` (non-top-level) expands to a `yin/def` of a macro\n   956\t  lambda with the requested policy.\n   957\t\n   958\tAcceptance: green on three hosts; `expand-batch` output is deterministic\n   959\t(two runs, equal vectors); a batch without macro nodes is returned unchanged\n   960\t(no root fact, no allocation beyond the scan).\n   961\t\n   962\t### Phase 2 — AST-walker runtime integration\n   963\t\n   964\tDeliverables: `ast_walker.cljc` — `:yin/macro-expand` arm in\n   965\t`cesk-transition`; `:lambda` arm copies macro flags; `apply-function` rejects\n   966\tmacro closures; loader records `:min-eid` and `:program-root` and keeps\n   967\t`:eid` on macro lambdas; `create-vm` accepts `:macro-eval`,\n   968\t`:macro-authorize`, `:capability`; `:macro-expansions` and `:macro-eid`\n   969\tfields; `macro/drain-expansions`. `yang.clojure`: `defmacro` policy\n   970\tmetadata; `compile-program` macro-env arity and `compile-program*`.\n   971\t\n   972\tTests: `ast_walker_test.cljc` additions:\n   973\t- A `:runtime` macro defined and called in one program expands and runs;\n   974\t  the ledger holds one event with `:yin/phase :runtime` and output datoms\n   975\t  with `m` = event.\n   976\t- A `:both` macro expanded at compile time leaves the ledger empty (zero\n   977\t  runtime overhead is observable).\n   978\t- No authorizer → `:unauthorized`; `(constantly true)` → expands; an\n   979\t  authorizer returning a map → that map in the error and on the event.\n   980\t- `:compile` macro reaching the transition (via `vm/eval` with no pass) →\n   981\t  `:phase-violation`.\n   982\t- A macro closure applied as a function → error.\n   983\t- A runtime macro inside a loop of 10³ iterations expands each time and the\n   984\t  ledger has 10³ events; `drain-expansions` empties it and returns them.\n   985\t- Park after an expansion, `pr-str`/`read-string` the VM value, resume: the\n   986\t  spliced AST is in the value; nothing is a host object.\n   987\t- Hot-loop parity: the countdown benchmark program's step count and value\n   988\t  are unchanged before and after this phase (the inlined loop did not change).\n   989\t\n   990\tAcceptance: green on three hosts; walker macro-free corpus unchanged;\n   991\tparity test docstring updated.\n   992\t\n   993\t### Phase 3 — Semantic VM runtime integration\n   994\t\n   995\tPrerequisite: semantic spec Phases 1–2 (the VM and `linearize` exist).\n   996\t\n   997\tDeliverables: `linearize.cljc` — `:macro-call` emission, `:closure` macro\n   998\tattributes, `lower-ast` options `:terminator :return`, `:derived-from`, `:m`,\n   999\t`:phase-violation` on `:compile` nodes; `semantic.cljc` — `:macro-call`\n  1000\ttransition (resolve, phase, authority, expand, lower, load, segment call),\n  1001\t`:macro-sites`, `:min-eid`, ledger fields, `create-vm` options as the\n  1002\twalker's; `yin.repl.core` — `make-session` composes\n  1003\t`(comp semantic/vm-load-program linearize/lower (macro/expand-with …))` for\n  1004\t`:semantic`.\n  1005\t\n  1006\tTests: `semantic_test.cljc` / `linearize_test.cljc` additions:\n  1007\t- Lowering a deferred macro yields exactly one `:macro-call` with an inline\n  1008\t  `:yin.code/call-ast` equal to the node map; a `:compile` node fails.\n  1009\t- The ephemeral segment ends in `:return`; its instructions carry `m` =\n  1010\t  event and `:yin.code/source` into the expansion output; it is present in\n  1011\t  the ledger entry.\n  1012\t- Non-tail `:macro-call`: `k` grows by one frame during the expansion and\n  1013\t  returns to `pc+1` with the expansion's value; tail `:macro-call` in a\n  1014\t  tail-recursive loop keeps `k` depth 0 over 10⁵ iterations.\n  1015\t- Parity axis: every Phase 2 walker macro test runs on `:semantic` with the\n  1016\t  closure normalization of the semantic spec §7.2; values, effect order,\n  1017\t  and ledger events (modulo the extra segment datoms) are equal.\n  1018\t- A shipped continuation captured inside an ephemeral segment resumes on a\n  1019\t  second VM that received the segment datoms ahead of it (the semantic\n  1020\t  spec's own handoff demo, with a macro).\n  1021\t\n  1022\tAcceptance: green on three hosts; the macro-free semantic corpus unchanged.\n  1023\t\n  1024\t### Phase 4 — Cross-language verification and tests\n  1025\t\n  1026\tDeliverables: `yang.python/compile` and `yang.php/compile` macro-env\n  1027\tarities with `:call` interception and shadowing; REPL `:macro-env`\n  1028\tpersistence and the single program path (§6.2); `(compile …)` rendering of\n  1029\texpansions; `python_macro_test.clj` and `php_macro_test.clj`.\n  1030\t\n  1031\tTests:\n  1032\t- The Python parser has no list literal, so a `defn(\"double\", [\"x\"], …)`\n  1033\t  call cannot be written today; the cross-language test therefore defines a\n  1034\t  small Clojure macro with a scalar signature,\n  1035\t  `(defmacro twice [f] (yin/application f [(yin/application f [(yin/literal 1)])]))`,\n  1036\t  and calls `twice(inc_fn)` from Python and `twice($inc_fn)` from PHP. The\n  1037\t  three frontends' `:yin/macro-expand` nodes, expanded in one batch after\n  1038\t  composing the streams as `docs/cross-language-macro.md` §4 prescribes,\n  1039\t  produce equal values on the walker and the semantic VM. A second test\n  1040\t  exercises `yin/name-of` with a Python string, a Clojure symbol, and a\n  1041\t  Clojure `:variable` reaching one macro. A `defn` from Python is reserved\n  1042\t  until the parser gains list literals.\n  1043\t- Original call datoms from the Python batch are present and unchanged\n  1044\t  after expansion; the event's `:yin/macro-name` is `twice` and\n  1045\t  `:yin/macro` refs the Clojure lambda's eid.\n  1046\t- REPL shell test: `(defmacro …)` then a call in the next input expands;\n  1047\t  `(lang :python)` then a call to the same macro expands; `(reset)` forgets\n  1048\t  it and the call fails `:unresolved`.\n  1049\t- REPL `(compile …)` shows expanded datoms and events.\n  1050\t\n  1051\tAcceptance: green on three hosts; `docs/cross-language-macro.md` status\n  1052\tupdated to \"implemented, Phase 4\"; divergence register's Macros section\n  1053\tmatches observed behaviour.\n  1054\t\n  1055\t### Reserved (not scheduled)\n  1056\t\n  1057\tRuntime expansion memo keyed by call site; expansion log as a stream\n  1058\tcomposed by the VM's constructor; Python/PHP `defmacro` surfaces over the\n  1059\tprelude; DaoDB-backed `macro-env` producer; Shibi authorizer; automatic\n  1060\thygiene; the stack VM's `OP_MACRO_EXPAND` (with the stack VM).\n  1061\t\n  1062\t---\n  1063\t\n  1064\t## Appendix A. Deviations from the brief and from the prior documents, with reasons\n  1065\t\n  1066\t1. **Macro arguments are AST maps, not eids** (vs v1 `yin.vm.macro` and the\n  1067\t   `ctx` of macro-design.md). Reason: every frontend and evaluator already\n  1068\t   speaks maps; eids coupled macro bodies to a datom index and to Clojure.\n  1069\t   Sharing of unchanged subtrees is preserved by carrying `:eid` (§3.5), so\n  1070\t   the provenance and datom-count properties v1 had are kept.\n  1071\t2. **Outermost-first expansion** (vs v1's post-order `transform`). Reason: a\n  1072\t   macro must be able to see and rewrite operand forms that contain macro\n  1073\t   calls; innermost-first makes `->`-style macros impossible. Fixpoint and\n  1074\t   guards are unaffected.\n  1075\t3. **Frontends do not coerce arguments for a macro's benefit** (vs\n  1076\t   `docs/cross-language-macro.md` §3). Reason: it couples a frontend to\n  1077\t   macro signatures it cannot know; the prelude accepts the canonical\n  1078\t   shapes instead.\n  1079\t4. **The root is an explicit fact** (new). Reason: non-destructive expansion\n  1080\t   leaves the old root unreferenced and the existing heuristic would run the\n  1081\t   unexpanded program. This is \"no assumed graphs\" applied to the batch.\n  1082\t5. **Runtime expansion in the semantic VM is a segment call, not a\n  1083\t   recompile-at-boundary** (vs macro-design.md \"Runtime flow\"). Reason: v2\n  1084\t   segments are immutable (semantic spec §5.2); `maybe-recompile-at-boundary`\n  1085\t   was v1's mechanism for programs that grow by append, and a spliced\n  1086\t   ephemeral segment is both simpler and exactly what a continuation can\n  1087\t   name.\n  1088\t6. **The expansion record is a VM-value ledger, not a stream append inside\n  1089\t   the transition** (interpreting \"explicit boundary effects\"). Reason: §4.4.\n  1090\t7. **`:yin/timestamp` is logical and optional** (the brief lists it as an\n  1091\t   event attribute). Reason: a host clock inside the expander destroys\n  1092\t   determinism and the parity test; §5.2.1.\n  1093\t8. **`:yin/macro-name` is added to the event.** Reason: bootstrap macros\n  1094\t   have no entity, and cross-batch `:yin/macro` refs are unresolvable until\n  1095\t   commit; a name makes both cases queryable.\n  1096\t9. **`eval` does not expand.** Reason: the divergence register already\n  1097\t   defines `eval` as direct evaluation of supplied work; composing the pass\n  1098\t   in front of it is one `comp`, and a hidden pass inside `eval` would be\n  1099\t   the implicit control flow the invariants forbid.\n  1100\t10. **`macro-eval` is supplied, not required by `macro`.** Reason: the require\n  1101\t    cycle `ast-walker → macro → ast-walker`, and the rule that evaluator\n  1102\t    choices belong to the composition.\n  1103\t\n  1104\t## Appendix B. Risks the implementer should watch\n  1105\t\n  1106\t- **Root selection.** Until Phase 0's `index-datoms` change lands, any\n  1107\t  expanded batch runs the *unexpanded* program silently. Land the root fact\n  1108\t  and its test first, before the expander exists.\n  1109\t- **`:eid` leakage into the walker's hot path.** `:keep-eids?` must be used\n  1110\t  only for macro lambdas in the loader and for operand reconstruction in the\n  1111\t  expander. An `:eid` on every node costs a map entry per node on the hot\n  1112\t  path and changes the `compile` command's rendered AST; the Phase 0\n  1113\t  acceptance test guards it.\n  1114\t- **Tail marking in `yin/make-lambda`.** v1's `mark-tail!` recursed through\n  1115\t  `:if` and immediately-applied lambdas; missing a case loses TCO in\n  1116\t  macro-generated bodies and shows up only as stack growth on deep\n  1117\t  recursion. The Phase 3 tail test (10⁵ iterations, `k` depth 0) is the\n  1118\t  guard; run it on the walker too.\n  1119\t- **Ephemeral segment ordering.** `lower-ast` for an expansion must emit the\n  1120\t  main sequence ending in `:return`, then out-of-line lambda bodies; a `:halt`\n  1121\t  left in by reuse of the program lowering path halts the outer VM inside a\n  1122\t  macro. Test by expanding a macro whose output contains a lambda.\n  1123\t- **Ledger growth.** A long-lived VM running a runtime macro in a loop\n  1124\t  accumulates events until the composition drains. The REPL drains after\n  1125\t  each evaluation; a daemon composition must drain on its own cadence. The\n  1126\t  10³-iteration test in Phase 2 documents the cost.\n  1127\t- **Authorizer absent in tests.** Every runtime-macro test must construct\n  1128\t  the VM with `:macro-authorize`; a test that forgets sees `:unauthorized`\n  1129\t  and may be \"fixed\" by adding a default. Do not add a default.\n  1130\t- **cljd `opcase` literal map** — `:macro-call 24` in both places, or the\n  1131\t  semantic VM dispatches a `:macro-call` to the default arm.\n  1132\t- **Outermost-first and `compile-macro-operands`.** yang.clojure's\n  1133\t  `:yang/shadow-params-operand` hint compiles later operands with macro\n  1134\t  names shadowed; that logic is unchanged, but it means the *frontend*\n  1135\t  decides some shadowing that an outermost-first expander would otherwise\n  1136\t  see as macro calls. Keep the hint semantics documented next to §3.3 so no\n  1137\t  one \"simplifies\" them away.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '190,520p' collab/1789222642509-architect-macro-system-v2-design.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '190,520p' collab/1789222642509-architect-macro-system-v2-design.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md\"","aggregated_output":"`(yin/def name <macro-lambda>)` therefore puts a macro closure in the store\nunder `name`, and a later `:yin/macro-expand` whose operator is\n`{:variable name}` resolves it with `engine/resolve-var` exactly like any\nvariable. The semantic VM's `:closure` instruction carries the same three\nfacts as operand attributes (`:yin.code/macro?`, `:yin.code/phase-policy`,\n`:yin.code/macro-ast`) — §4.2 explains why the last one is inlined.\n\n### 2.4 The argument protocol and the macro prelude\n\n**Binding.** `(bind-params params operand-asts)` produces the env for the\nmacro body: positional params bind one AST map each; `& rest` binds a vector\nof the remaining AST maps; arity mismatch is a hard expansion error naming\nthe call eid (`{:kind :arity :expected n :got m}`). This binding is the\nexpander's, so the walker's `zipmap` closure application (no `&`) is not\ninvolved and does not change.\n\n**Return.** The body's value must be a Universal AST map. `macro/valid-ast?`\nwalks it and requires: every node a map with a keyword `:type` in the\ncanonical set (`:literal :variable :lambda :application :if\n:dao.stream.apply/call :vm/* :stream/* :yin/macro-expand`); ref-valued keys\n(`:body :operator :test :consequent :alternate :target :val :source`) hold\nmaps; `:operands` a vector of maps; `:params` a vector of symbols. Any\nother value is a hard error (`{:kind :invalid-output}`) — no coercion of a\nbare number or symbol into a literal, because \"no silent fallback\" is the\nrule and the prelude makes constructing the right node one call.\n\n**The prelude** is the set of primitives merged into the throwaway VM's\n`:primitives` for the duration of one macro-body invocation. They are pure\nconstructors and accessors over AST maps:\n\n| Primitive | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `yin/node-type ast` | `(:type ast)` |\n| `yin/node-get ast k` | `(get ast k)`; the only accessor a macro needs |\n| `yin/literal v`, `yin/variable sym` | leaf constructors |\n| `yin/lambda params body`, `yin/application op operands`, `yin/if t c a` | node constructors; `params` is a vector of symbols |\n| `yin/sequence-body [ast ...]` | the `do` desugaring yang uses (nested `(fn [_] ...)` applications); empty → `nil` literal |\n| `yin/make-lambda params-ast body-ast` | compatibility with the v1 stdlib: `params-ast` is a `:literal` whose value is the param vector; marks tail positions in `body-ast` exactly as v1's `mark-tail!` did (application and macro-expand nodes in tail position of the body, recursing through `:if` branches and immediately-applied lambdas) so tail calls in macro-generated bodies still get the walker's TCO |\n| `yin/make-def name-ast value-ast` | `(yin/def <sym> value)` application; `name-ast` may be `:variable`, a `:literal` symbol, or a `:literal` string (§2.6 on why all three) |\n| `yin/name-of ast` | the symbol named by any of those three shapes; error otherwise |\n| `yin/macro-call op-ast operands` | constructs a `:yin/macro-expand` node — how a macro emits a call to another (or the same) macro |\n| `yin/gensym-sym prefix` | a fresh symbol `prefix__<E>_<n>` where `E` is the absolute value of the expansion-event eid and `n` an invocation-local counter; unique across expansions without cross-invocation state |\n\nPlus the ordinary `yin.vm/primitives` (arithmetic, `first`, `rest`,\n`conj`, `assoc`, `get`, `vec`, `=`, `nil?`, `empty?`) so a macro can walk and\nbuild vectors. `yin/def` inside a macro body writes to the throwaway store\nand is discarded; `require` resolves against the throwaway VM's `:modules`,\nwhich is `nil` — a macro body that needs a module is an error, by design.\n\nThe throwaway VM is built with `:make-stream` absent and no FFI pair: a\nmacro body that reaches a stream or FFI effect fails with the existing\n\"constructed without :make-stream\" / \"no call pair\" errors. A macro body that\nparks (`:vm/park`) or blocks is an expansion error `{:kind :suspended}`.\nMacros are total, synchronous transformations of syntax; anything else is a\nfunction.\n\n**Hygiene** is v1's: non-hygienic by default, `yin/gensym-sym` on request.\nFull hygiene stays out of scope.\n\n### 2.5 `yang.clojure`\n\nAlready emits the representation above; three changes:\n\n1. `compile-program` gains a third arity taking an initial `macro-env`\n   (default `initial-macro-env`), so a caller that has macros from earlier\n   units (the REPL across evaluations, a build across files) can seed it.\n   `compile-program` also *returns* the final macro-env when asked\n   (`compile-program* forms tail? macro-env → {:ast :macro-env}`), so the\n   caller can persist it. The existing arities are unchanged.\n2. Non-top-level `(defmacro ...)` goes through the bootstrap `defmacro`\n   macro (`:yin/macro-expand` with operator `{:variable defmacro}` and\n   operands `[name params body*]`), and the expander's bootstrap table\n   expands it to `(yin/def name <lambda macro? true>)` — v1's `defmacro-fn`\n   over AST maps instead of eids. Top-level `defmacro` stays lowered directly\n   as today. A `defmacro` whose name symbol carries `^{:phase-policy\n   :runtime}` (or `:both`) metadata sets the lambda's policy; the default\n   stays `:compile`.\n3. `stdlib-forms` (the `defn` macro) moves from the deleted `yin.vm.macro`\n   to `yin.vm.macro/stdlib-forms`, unchanged in text; yang's docstring\n   already points there by name.\n\n### 2.6 Non-homoiconic frontends (`yang.python`, `yang.php`)\n\n`compile` gains an arity `(compile source macro-env)` where `macro-env` is a\nmap `symbol → {:type :user :eid e :lambda-ast ast}` (the same shape yang.clojure\nthreads). In `compile-stmt`, a `:call` whose function is a `:variable` naming\na key of `macro-env` emits\n\n```\n{:type :yin/macro-expand\n :operator {:type :variable :name sym}\n :operands (mapv #(compile-stmt % false) args)}\n```\n\ninstead of `:application`. Names shadowed by an enclosing `lambda`/`def`\nparameter are removed from the env for that scope, as yang.clojure does.\n\n**Normalization is what the frontend already does.** `compile-stmt` on each\nargument produces a canonical node: a Python identifier is a `:variable`, a\nstring is a `:literal` string, a number a `:literal`, a lambda a `:lambda`.\n`docs/cross-language-macro.md` §3 proposed that the frontend additionally\nlower `\"my_func\"` to a `:variable` node for `defn`'s benefit. This design\nrejects that: a frontend must not know a macro's signature (that is a hidden\ncoupling from syntax to a particular macro), and it cannot know it for a\nmacro defined in another language. Instead the prelude's `yin/make-def` and\n`yin/name-of` accept a `:variable`, a `:literal` symbol, or a `:literal`\nstring. A Python `defn(\"my_func\", ...)` and a Clojure `(defn my-func ...)`\ntherefore reach the same macro with different canonical shapes, and the macro\nis written to accept both — which is a one-line accessor, not a frontend\nrule. The contract for macro authors: *arguments arrive as whatever canonical\nnode the caller's frontend produces for that source form; use the prelude\naccessors, not `:type` pattern matching, for names.*\n\nPython and PHP remain *consumers* of macros in this design. Authoring from\nthose languages (`docs/cross-language-macro.md` §2, the direct AST API) is\nthe prelude itself — a Python program can call `yin.make_lambda(...)` once a\nPython `defmacro` surface exists — and is reserved; nothing here prevents it.\n\n### 2.7 Discovery: local and cross-unit\n\nA frontend needs to know which names are macros *before* emitting. Two\nsources, one shape:\n\n- **Same-unit (local).** yang.clojure's threaded `macro-env` today; the\n  Python/PHP frontends have no macro-defining form, so their local env is\n  always what they were given.\n- **Cross-unit.** The composition supplies `macro-env`. Where it comes from\n  is the composition's business: the REPL builds it by scanning each\n  evaluated batch (`macro/definitions datoms → {sym {:eid :lambda-ast\n  :phase-policy}}`, which finds `(yin/def sym <lambda macro? true>)`\n  applications, last-write-wins in batch order, exactly v1's\n  `find-macro-lambda-by-name` inverted into a map); a build tool builds it\n  from previously committed streams; DaoDB, when it can answer\n  `[?e :yin/macro? true]` joined to its `yin/def`, is a third producer of the\n  same map. No frontend queries anything itself.\n\nThe expander resolves a `:variable` operator by the same env plus the batch\nscan, in this order: batch definitions (same-unit wins) → supplied\n`:macro-env` → bootstrap table (`defmacro`) → error `{:kind :unresolved\n:name sym :call eid}`. At runtime (§4) the store replaces the first two.\n\n---\n\n## §3. The compile-time expander (`yin.vm.macro`)\n\n### 3.1 Contract\n\n```\nexpand-batch : datoms opts → {:datoms datoms' :root-eid r :events [eid ...] :deferred [eid ...]}\nexpand       : datoms opts → datoms'                    ; = (:datoms (expand-batch ...))\ndefinitions  : datoms → {sym {:eid :lambda-ast :phase-policy}}\ninvoke       : macro-ast operand-asts ctx → ast          ; one macro body, §3.2\nvalid-ast?   : value → nil | {:kind :invalid-output :at path}\nstdlib-forms : the defn macro source\nbootstrap    : {'defmacro (fn [operand-asts ctx] → ast)}\n```\n\n`opts`: `:macro-eval` (required — the composition supplies it; see §3.2),\n`:macro-env` (default `{}`), `:bootstrap` (default `bootstrap`), `:phase`\n(default `:compile`), `:max-depth` 100, `:max-datoms` 10000, `:t` (the\ntransaction slot written on emitted datoms, default 0), `:authorize`\n(default `(constantly true)` at compile time — §5.3).\n\n`datoms'` is `datoms` followed by the appended event and output datoms; the\ninput vector is a prefix of the output, byte for byte. Pure: same inputs,\nsame output, on every host.\n\n### 3.2 Executing the macro lambda\n\n`invoke` builds `env = (bind-params params operand-asts)` and calls\n\n```\n(:macro-eval opts) lambda-ast env prelude → value\n```\n\nwhere the composition's `:macro-eval` is, for every current composition,\n`yin.vm.ast-walker/macro-eval`:\n\n```\n(defn macro-eval [lambda-ast env prelude]\n  (let [vm (create-vm {:env env\n                       :primitives (merge vm/primitives prelude)})\n        result (vm/eval vm (:body lambda-ast))]\n    (cond (vm/blocked? result) (throw (ex-info \"Macro body suspended\" {...}))\n          (not (vm/halted? result)) (throw ...)\n          :else (vm/value result))))\n```\n\n`yin.vm.macro` does **not** require `yin.vm.ast-walker`; the walker\nrequires `macro` (for its runtime branch) and exports `macro-eval` for\ncompositions to hand back in. This breaks the require cycle and keeps the\nchoice of body evaluator where every other evaluator choice lives — the\ncomposition (`yin.repl.core/make-vm`, test utilities, build tools). A\ncomposition may supply any function of the same signature; the semantic VM\nis *not* an acceptable default for it (decision 2), but a host that ports\nonly a walker can still expand macros.\n\nThe throwaway VM is constructed per invocation. Construction cost (a record\nand a map) is negligible next to running a body, and per-invocation\nconstruction is what makes the \"no shared mutable state\" row of §1.2 true\nwithout argument.\n\n### 3.3 The algorithm\n\nPost-order over the *tree reachable from the root*, with outermost-first\nexpansion at each macro node and immediate re-expansion of the result:\n\n```\nexpand-node ctx eid :\n  node-type = type(eid)\n  if node-type = :yin/macro-expand and enabled?(ctx, macro(eid)):\n      guard depth\n      operands  = [reconstruct ctx o | o ∈ operands(eid)]      ; AST maps with :eid kept\n      macro     = resolve ctx operator(eid)                    ; §2.7\n      event-eid = fresh ctx\n      out-ast   = invoke macro operands ctx'                   ; ctx' carries event-eid for gensym\n      check valid-ast? out-ast\n      [root', ctx''] = emit ctx' out-ast m=event-eid           ; §3.5\n      ctx''' = emit-event ctx'' event-eid eid macro root'\n      guard datoms\n      return expand-node (ctx''' with depth+1) root'           ; re-expand the output\n  else:\n      for each ref attribute a of eid, child c:\n          [c', ctx] = expand-node ctx c\n      if any c' ≠ c: copy eid to fresh eid' with substituted refs, m = default-op\n      return [eid or eid', ctx]\n```\n\nProperties, each of which is a test in Phase 1:\n\n- **Fixpoint by construction.** Every node reachable from the final root has\n  been visited after its last rewrite; a `:yin/macro-expand` remains only if\n  `enabled?` was false for it (a `:runtime`-policy macro at compile time),\n  and each such node is listed in `:deferred`. A final scan of the output\n  tree asserts this and is cheap (linear in the output).\n- **Outermost first.** `operands` are reconstructed *unexpanded*. A macro\n  such as `->` or `when-let` that rewrites forms containing other macro calls\n  sees them as written. This differs from v1's innermost-first `transform`\n  (Appendix A.2).\n- **Depth** counts nested re-expansion: a macro whose output contains a macro\n  call whose output contains a macro call ... is depth 3. Direct recursion\n  (`(defmacro loop* ... (yin/macro-call loop* ...))`) without a base case\n  hits the guard at 100 with `{:kind :depth-guard :depth 100 :chain [eid ...]}`\n  — the chain of call eids is in the error, so the offending macro is\n  nameable from the error alone. Sibling expansions do not accumulate depth.\n- **Sharing.** An operand that a macro returns unchanged (the same map, still\n  carrying its `:eid`) is referenced, not copied (§3.5). The typical `defn`\n  expansion therefore emits the new `yin/def` application and lambda nodes\n  and *refs* the body the user wrote; only the ancestor chain from the call\n  site up to the root is copied, as in v1.\n- **Phase.** `enabled?` is: `:compile` or `:both` when `(:phase opts)` is\n  `:compile`; `:runtime` or `:both` when it is `:runtime` (the runtime callers\n  in §4 use the same function with `:phase :runtime` and a single node). A\n  `:compile` macro encountered with `:phase :runtime` is a hard error\n  `{:kind :phase-violation}`: it means the compile pass was skipped, and\n  silently expanding it at runtime would hide that.\n\n### 3.4 Guards and failure\n\n| Guard | Default | Error data |\n|---|---|---|\n| depth | 100 | `{:kind :depth-guard :depth d :chain [call-eid ...]}` |\n| emitted datoms per `expand-batch` call | 10,000 | `{:kind :datom-guard :emitted n :call call-eid}` |\n| arity | — | `{:kind :arity :macro eid :expected :got}` |\n| output shape | — | `{:kind :invalid-output :macro eid :at [path]}` |\n| unresolved macro | — | `{:kind :unresolved :name sym :call eid}` |\n| phase | — | `{:kind :phase-violation :macro eid :policy p :phase ph}` |\n| suspended body | — | `{:kind :suspended :macro eid}` |\n| authority | — | `{:kind :unauthorized :macro eid :phase ph}` |\n\nEvery failure is thrown as `ex-info` whose data is the map above under\n`:yin/error`, **and** — when an event eid was already minted — the expander\nfirst appends the event entity with `:yin/error <that map>` and no\n`:yin/expansion-root`, so a failed expansion is queryable (macro-design.md\nlists `:yin/error` as an event attribute for this reason). `expand-batch`\nitself does not return partial output: the throw carries `:datoms-so-far`\nfor diagnosis only.\n\n### 3.5 Output, the root fact, and eid discipline\n\n**Emission.** `emit` is `vm/ast->datoms-with-root` with three options that\nPhase 0 adds to `yin.vm`:\n\n- `:m event-eid` — already supported; every output datom carries it.\n- `:id-start` — already supported; the context's next fresh eid, below the\n  batch minimum (v1's `make-eid-counter!` rule: strictly below every eid in\n  the input so tempids never collide).\n- `:existing #{eid ...}` — **new**: a node carrying `:eid` in this set is\n  returned as a reference and *not* re-emitted. This is the sharing rule.\n  `datoms->ast` gains the inverse option `:keep-eids? true` and the expander\n  reconstructs operands with it. `vm-load-program` uses `:keep-eids?` only\n  for macro lambdas (so closures can carry `:eid`, §2.3) — ordinary nodes stay\n  eid-free on the walker's hot path and in the `compile` command's output.\n\n**The root fact.** After the top-level `expand-node` returns `root'`, the\nexpander appends `[root' :yin/root true t default-op]` (retracting nothing —\nan older `:yin/root` on the original root, if a previous pass wrote one, is\nsuperseded by \"last `:yin/root` fact in batch order wins\"). `index-datoms`\ngains: if any `:yin/root` fact exists, the root is the entity of the last one;\notherwise the existing heuristic, restricted to entities whose `:yin/type` is\nnot `:macro-expand-event`. Both are one-line changes in `yin.vm` and the\nsecond is required even for batches with no `:yin/root`, because an event\nentity is typed and unreferenced and would otherwise win the heuristic.\n`:yin/root` joins `schema` as a boolean.\n\n**Eid discipline across batches.** Within one `expand-batch` call, all refs\nare within one batch and valid as tempids. A macro resolved through\n`:macro-env` from another unit has an eid that belongs to that unit's batch;\nthe event's `:yin/macro` ref is then meaningful only once both batches are\ncommitted to the same DaoDB with tempids resolved — the pipeline\n`docs/cross-language-macro.md` §4 describes. The expander records the fact\nhonestly and also writes `:yin/macro-name sym` on the event, so the link\nis recoverable by name even when the ref is cross-batch. `:yin/macro-name`\nis the only attribute this design adds to the event beyond macro-design.md.\n\n### 3.6 Feeding the three evaluators\n\nThe output of `expand-batch` is a batch of `:yin/*` datoms with an explicit\nroot and no compile-enabled macro nodes. Each consumer takes it unchanged:\n\n1. **`yin.vm.ast-walker`**: `vm-load-program` calls `datoms->ast`, which\n   now honours `:yin/root`; the walker never sees a `:yin/macro-expand`\n   node unless the batch carried a `:runtime`/`:both` macro that was\n   deferred — and then it takes the §4.1 transition. Composition:\n   `(comp ast-walker/vm-load-program (macro/expand-with opts))` handed to\n   `observer/run-on-stream`, or `macro/expand` applied to `(vm/ast->datoms\n   ast)` before `vm-load-program` in a direct evaluation path.\n2. **`yin.vm.linearize` → `yin.vm.semantic`**: `lower` reads the same\n   root fact; its existing rejection of `:yin/macro-expand` becomes the\n   §4.2 rule (reject `:compile`-policy nodes with `:phase-violation`, emit\n   `:macro-call` for the rest). Composition: `(comp semantic/vm-load-program\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
